1
00:00:00,866 --> 00:00:03,016
You've built a fantastic website.

2
00:00:03,016 --> 00:00:05,516
It works perfectly for your local audience.

3
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:07,800
Now, you want to go global.

4
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:11,600
So you hire a translator, swap out the text

5
00:00:11,616 --> 00:00:13,483
and you're done, right?

6
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:15,916
Unfortunately, it's not that simple.

7
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:18,316
If you've ever seen something like this

8
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:21,500
you've seen a failure of Internationalization.

9
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:23,650
Hi, I'm Fuqiao Xue

10
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:26,016
and I lead the World Wide Web Consortium's

11
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,166
Internationalization Activity

12
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:31,633
At the W3C, we help create the standards

13
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:35,166
that make the web work for everyone, everywhere.

14
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:39,300
In this video, I'm going to introduce two core concepts:

15
00:00:40,266 --> 00:00:42,033
Internationalization and Localization.

16
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:45,416
Let's start with the big one

17
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,866
Internationalization. We often shorten it to

18
00:00:48,866 --> 00:00:49,800
'i18n'

19
00:00:49,983 --> 00:00:52,716
that's 'i', then 18 letters

20
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:55,833
then 'n'. It's a bit of developer shorthand.

21
00:00:55,966 --> 00:00:56,616
i18n

22
00:00:56,616 --> 00:01:00,266
is the process of designing your code and content

23
00:01:00,616 --> 00:01:03,716
so it can be easily adapted to various languages

24
00:01:04,016 --> 00:01:06,216
writing systems, and regions

25
00:01:06,466 --> 00:01:08,783
Now, what about Localization?

26
00:01:09,116 --> 00:01:12,100
We call that 'L10n'

27
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:14,350
'L', 10 letters, 'n'

28
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:15,566
Localization

29
00:01:15,566 --> 00:01:18,616
is the process of actually adapting your product

30
00:01:18,716 --> 00:01:21,566
for a specific market, or 'locale'.

31
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:23,866
This is where translation happens.

32
00:01:24,366 --> 00:01:26,700
But it is also where you adapt graphics

33
00:01:26,966 --> 00:01:30,666
change currencies, and use the correct local formats

34
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,783
for things like dates and addresses.

35
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:34,733
So, the key difference:

36
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:36,683
Internationalization is designing for potential.

37
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:41,650
Localization is delivering for reality.

38
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:43,583
So what does this foundation

39
00:01:43,750 --> 00:01:46,850
this internationalization actually include?

40
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:50,050
It's so much more than just leaving space for text.

41
00:01:50,116 --> 00:01:52,300
first character encoding

42
00:01:52,783 --> 00:01:55,966
if your system doesn't support the global character set

43
00:01:56,266 --> 00:01:59,800
Unicode text in languages like Japanese

44
00:01:59,983 --> 00:02:04,483
Hindi or Arabic can turn into gibberish second

45
00:02:04,883 --> 00:02:06,383
style and layout

46
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:10,700
some languages like German have much longer words

47
00:02:10,916 --> 00:02:13,583
others like Arabic or Hebrew

48
00:02:13,633 --> 00:02:15,533
are written from right to left

49
00:02:15,916 --> 00:02:16,600
a well

50
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,016
internationalized site adapts its layout automatically

51
00:02:20,116 --> 00:02:21,933
third forms

52
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:25,450
not everyone has a first name and a last name

53
00:02:25,716 --> 00:02:28,900
in Hungary the family name comes first

54
00:02:29,116 --> 00:02:31,783
in Iceland people may use patronymics

55
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,316
a global ready form needs to be flexible

56
00:02:35,633 --> 00:02:39,650
and this applies to so much more like dates and numbers

57
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,516
Is this December 1st or January 2th?

58
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:46,500
And how you write prices is critical.

59
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,516
In the US this is one thousand

60
00:02:49,516 --> 00:02:52,850
two hundred thirty four dollars and fifty six cents

61
00:02:52,916 --> 00:02:54,250
But in Germany

62
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:56,816
the same price would be written like this

63
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:00,650
with the separator swapped and using the Euro symbol

64
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:02,233
If you get this wrong

65
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:04,933
you're displaying a completely different number.

66
00:03:05,316 --> 00:03:08,516
It's a small detail that makes a world of difference

67
00:03:08,516 --> 00:03:09,416
for user trust.

68
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:11,316
Internationalization also applies to

69
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:13,933
web addresses

70
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:17,083
thanks to Internationalized Resource Identifiers

71
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:20,466
or IRIs, web addresses can include characters

72
00:03:20,466 --> 00:03:22,566
from scripts all around the world

73
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:24,333
Let's put it all together.

74
00:03:24,433 --> 00:03:26,633
A poorly internationalized website

75
00:03:26,783 --> 00:03:29,133
when localized for Saudi Arabia

76
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,166
might just translate the text

77
00:03:31,233 --> 00:03:32,666
The layout will be broken

78
00:03:32,666 --> 00:03:34,533
because it's still left to right

79
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:36,950
the prices will be in dollars

80
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:39,033
and the date format will be confusing

81
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:41,400
But a well internationalized website

82
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,566
when localized, looks and feels native

83
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:46,466
the layout is right to left

84
00:03:46,466 --> 00:03:48,650
the currency is Saudi Riyals

85
00:03:48,866 --> 00:03:51,800
the calendar uses the local Hijri system

86
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,000
and the language is perfect.

87
00:03:54,066 --> 00:03:57,250
That's the power of thinking globally from the start.

88
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:00,100
You build one flexible system, that's i18n

89
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:04,133
that can support dozens of local experiences

90
00:04:04,266 --> 00:04:05,383
that's l10n.

91
00:04:05,383 --> 00:04:07,183
Making the web work for everyone

92
00:04:07,183 --> 00:04:09,800
is at the heart of the W3C's mission

93
00:04:10,233 --> 00:04:12,283
It's not just about technology

94
00:04:12,666 --> 00:04:15,650
it's about connecting people and cultures

95
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:18,966
by building a solid internationalization foundation

96
00:04:19,383 --> 00:04:23,000
you're showing respect for users around the world

97
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,366
Thanks for watching.

